{
  // WCH CH32V003: one QingKe V2A core (RV32EC, up to 48 MHz), 16 KB of flash, 2 KB of SRAM, no heap. Milestone M5. The
  // facts below come from WCH's documents (wch-ic.com), named where each is used: the CH32V003 Reference Manual (RM,
  // CH32V003RM V1.5), the CH32V003 Datasheet (DS, CH32V003DS0 V1.8), the QingKe V2 Microprocessor Manual (QKV2,
  // QingKeV2_Processor_Manual V1.1), and WCH's own start-up and system files for the chip (openwch/ch32v003,
  // EVT/EXAM/SRC: Startup/startup_ch32v00x.S, Ld/Link.ld, system_ch32v00x.c). Its registers are WCH's SVD
  // (chips/svd/CH32V003xx.svd), its pins and the rest ChipSharp's description of the chip (chips/ch32v003.json), from
  // which src/ChipSharp.Chips makes the runtime's headers (ch32v003_registers.h, ...).
  //
  // The processor: RV32EC (RM overview, "QingKe V2A core, RV32EC instruction set"; QKV2 1.1): RV32E, 16 registers, with
  // C, Zicsr, machine mode only; no M (no Zmmul either: QKV2 lists RV32, E, C and XW alone), so the code multiplies
  // and divides with the runtime's helpers; no A, F or D. XW, WCH's own 16-bit encodings of byte and half-word loads
  // and stores (c.lbu, c.lhu, c.sb, c.sh and their sp forms: QKV2 1.1; clang's Xwchc), would save 1.2% of a program's
  // code and is not used (see "march" below). A load or store at an address not a multiple of its size is an exception
  // (QKV2 Table 2-1, codes 4 and 6), so unalignedAccess stays false.
  //
  // Memory (RM 1.2, Figure 1-2): flash 16 KB at 0x08000000, also at 0x00000000 when the chip boots from it (the alias;
  // the system bootloader, 1920 bytes at 0x1FFFF000, is there instead when the option bytes' START_MODE asks for it: RM
  // 16.5); SRAM 2 KB at 0x20000000. The program is linked at the flash itself, 0x08000000-0x08003fff, where a
  // programmer writes it (the image "bin" takes the bytes from there); the core starts at the alias, and the vector
  // table's first instructions jump to the reset handler at its flash address, so the program runs where it is linked
  // (its PC-relative addresses, gp's and sp's, are right only there). WCH's own Link.ld links at the alias instead.
  //
  // Reset and interrupts. The core starts at 0x00000000 (RM Table 6-1, entry 0). The PFIC (RM 6, QKV2 3; 0xE000E000)
  // has 39 vector numbers here: 2 NMI, 3 HardFault (every exception), 12 SysTick, 14 SW, 16-38 the peripherals' 23;
  // mcause has the number (bit 31 set for an interrupt; an exception's code, 0-11, with bit 31 clear). mtvec (QKV2 2.2,
  // 3.2): MODE0 (bit 0) 1 makes an interrupt's entry mtvec's base + 4 * its number, MODE1 (bit 1) 1 makes the table hold
  // the handlers' addresses (not jump instructions), and the base must be 1 KB aligned; after reset mtvec is 0. So the
  // vector table is the first 156 bytes of flash ("link.head", ch32v003_vector_table.S): entries 0 and 1, where the
  // core starts (no interrupt has the number 1), are an absolute jump to chipsharp_reset (lui, jalr: WCH's start-up has
  // "j handle_reset" there, which stays at the alias), and entries 2-38 the address of the riscv32 runtime's trap
  // entry, chipsharp_trap; the chip's first steps put mtvec at the table (0x08000000 | 3, WCH's own MODE 11). Every
  // interrupt and exception then comes to chipsharp_trap, which saves the registers and dispatches by mcause to
  // chipsharp_isr_irq<n> ("vectors": 39), as on every RISC-V chip of ChipSharp.
  // HPE, the hardware prologue/epilogue (QKV2 3.4: INTSYSCR.HWSTKEN, CSR 0x804, which also has INESTEN, the nesting),
  // is off: its handlers must be compiled by WCH's toolchain as interrupt("WCH-Interrupt-fast"), which clang has not, and
  // the trap entry saves the same ten registers itself. Both bits are 0 after reset (RM 6.5.3.1), and the start-up
  // writes 0 to INTSYSCR all the same (WCH's start-up turns both on, and a bootloader may have): handlers do not nest,
  // and as the first level is then the last, the core clears mstatus.MIE on the way in (QKV2 2.2: MIE is not cleared
  // before the last level), as the trap entry expects.
  //
  // The clock (RM 3): after reset the system clock is the HSI, 24 MHz, and the AHB prescaler (RCC_CFGR0.HPRE, reset 0010)
  // divides it by 3: the core runs at 8 MHz. The chip's first steps (ch32v003_boot.c, WCH's SetSysClockTo_48MHZ_HSI)
  // raise it to 48 MHz: flash 1 wait state (FLASH_ACTLR.LATENCY 01, for 24-48 MHz: RM 16.3.1), HPRE 0000 (HCLK =
  // SYSCLK), the PLL from the HSI (PLLSRC 0, which multiplies by 2), PLLON and wait for PLLRDY, SW 10 (the PLL) and
  // wait for SWS 10. HSITRIM keeps its reset value (16: 24 MHz +- 1%).
  //
  // What runs at reset: neither watchdog. The IWDG is off after a reset unless the option bytes' IWDG_SW is 0 (RM 4.2.1,
  // 16.5: then the hardware starts it, and nothing stops it); ChipSharp neither reads nor changes the option bytes,
  // and the runtime does not feed a watchdog. The WWDG is off after a reset (RM 5.2.1). The 1-wire debug interface (SWIO, PD1) is on
  // after every reset (DS 1.4.18).
  //
  // What the runtime has (src/ChipSharp.Runtime.Ch32v003): the start-up, the clock at 48 MHz, the trap's dispatch, the
  // helpers (M5's wave 0); the pins and their pulls, SysTick's delays, clock and timer event, the pin event, USART1 as
  // the console and serial port 0 and its event (wave 1). I2C, SPI, the ADC and PWM come with wave 2: the capabilities
  // below say what is there, and chipsharp check refuses a program that uses the rest.
  //
  // How the runtime uses the chip (ch32v003_chip.h has the whole of it):
  //   - SysTick (RM 6.5.4, QKV2 4) counts at HCLK / 8, 6 MHz, from 0 at start-up, in 32 bits the runtime makes 64 (it
  //     counts the rounds whenever it reads the count, at least every 2^31 counts: its compare's interrupt guards it);
  //     its one compare is armed for the nearest of a delay's end and the timer event's time. The delays sleep (wfi)
  //     until it; the clock and Stopwatch are its count, 6 a microsecond. TIM1 and TIM2 stay free for PWM (wave 2).
  //   - The pins by port * 8 + bit (PA1-PA2 1-2, PC0-PC7 16-23, PD0-PD7 24-31). PD1 is SWIO, the one debug wire a
  //     WCH-LinkE programs the chip through, and PD7 NRST (the option bytes' RST_MODE): the runtime leaves both alone (as
  //     a pin the chip does not have) and chipsharp check refuses them ("pinFunctions"). Pull.Up and Pull.Down: CFGLR's
  //     input with a pull, OUTDR's bit choosing up or down (RM 7).
  //   - The pin event: EXTI's lines 0-7, line n for pin n of the port AFIO_EXTICR names, so one pin of each number is
  //     watched at a time (PC3 or PD3, not both: the program stops, saying so, when it watches the second).
  //   - USART1 on PD5 (TX) and PD6 (RX), its default pins, 8N1 at 48 MHz / BRR (733 to 3000000 baud; 115200 is BRR 417,
  //     0.08% slow): the console (Console.Write, Console.Read, the failures' messages) and serial port 0, with a 16-byte
  //     receive buffer its interrupt fills.
  //   - The program's end: what USART1 still sends goes out, then interrupts off (mstatus.MIE and the PFIC's): the wfi of
  //     the stop sleeps for good, and the program's events end with it, as on the Uno.
  "name": "ch32v003",
  "inherits": "riscv32e",
  "runtime": "ch32v003",
  // rv32ec_zicsr (riscv32e's). Not xwchc (clang's -march=rv32ec_zicsr_xwchc): on the 89 programs of the corpora that
  // fit the chip it takes 1.2% off the code (0.1% to 2.3% a program: the compressed byte and half-word loads and
  // stores), not worth an encoding every simulator and disassembler would need to know
  "march": "rv32ec_zicsr",
  "memory": {
    // the flash, where the program is linked and runs (the core starts at its alias, 0x00000000: see above); the SRAM
    "flash": { "origin": "0x08000000", "size": "16K" },
    "sram": { "origin": "0x20000000", "size": "2K" }
  },
  "link": {
    "code": "flash",
    "data": "sram",
    // a quarter of the 2 KB, as the Uno keeps of its 2 KB: the RAM the variables must leave for the stack, which has all
    // they leave (there is no heap)
    "stack": "512",
    // where the functions that check the stack (the recursive ones, those with frames of 256 bytes or more) stop the
    // program with "Stack overflow.": this far above the variables, not a quarter of "stack" (128 bytes), as the deepest
    // the runtime goes below such a function is the text of a double, some 790 bytes on RV32E (chipsharp_float_text.c's
    // big numbers take a frame of 668), and an interrupt's 40-byte frame and its handler may come on top of that. With
    // 128, a recursion that printed a double near the limit ran through the variables, and the console's among them
    "stackMargin": 896,
    // the vector table, at the start of flash (0x08000000, 1 KB aligned as mtvec needs): 39 entries of 4 bytes
    "head": [ { "section": ".ch32v003_vectors", "size": 156 } ],
    // the trap entry: the table holds its address, so 4 bytes (riscv32's 64 is for a CLIC's mtvec)
    "vectorAlign": 4
  },
  // the entries of the trap's dispatch (riscv32 runtime): the PFIC's vector numbers 0-38, chipsharp_isr_irq0 to 38
  "vectors": 39,
  "interrupts": {
    // the events an [Interrupt] method can handle. The pin event's is the PFIC's vector number of its source (RM Table
    // 6-1), whose handler is the method's entry, which runs the runtime's clear function first. The timer's and the
    // uart's are past the PFIC's 39 (as the Uno's and the STM8S103's): their sources are the runtime's own, SysTick's
    // compare (vector 12, shared with the delays and the count's guard) and USART1's receiving (vector 32, which fills
    // the buffer whether or not a program watches the port); the runtime's handlers of those find out whether the event
    // happened, then call the method's entry, an ordinary function, chipsharp_isr_irq<n> by the numbers here
    "timer": { "irq": 39 },                                       // SysTick's compare, at the times Every and After give
    "pin": { "irq": 20, "clear": "chipsharp_clear_pin_event" },   // EXTI7_0: EXTI lines 0-7, a pin each (AFIO_EXTICR)
    "uart": { "irq": 40 }                                         // USART1, once the bytes are in a watched port's buffer
  },
  "capabilities": {
    "heap": false,          // 2 KB of SRAM: no objects
    "pullDown": true,       // an input pulls up or down (CFGLR's input with a pull, OUTDR's bit choosing which)
    "console": true,        // Console.Write and Console.WriteLine go to USART1 (PD5, 115200 baud)
    "consoleInput": true,   // Console.Read reads USART1 (PD6, 115200 baud); ReadLine makes a string, which needs a heap
    "uart": 1,              // serial ports for Uart: USART1 (TX PD5, RX PD6)
    "clock": true,          // a clock for DateTime.Now and Stopwatch: SysTick's count, 6 a microsecond
    // with M5's wave 2: I2C1, SPI1, ADC1 and PWM on TIM1 and TIM2
    "i2c": false,
    "spi": false,
    "adc": 0,
    "pwm": 0
  },
  "pinFunctions": {
    // the pins a program may use as plain pins and watch: all but PD1 (SWIO) and PD7 (NRST), which chipsharp check
    // refuses as constants (the runtime leaves them alone, whatever the number)
    "pin": [1, 2, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30],
    "pinEvent": [1, 2, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30],
    // USART1's pins without a remap (AFIO_PCFR1's USART1_RM 0), ChipSharp's serial port 0
    "uart0.tx": [29], "uart0.rx": [30]
  }
}
